"""Draft for Compulsory Book 1, Chapter 4 and Compulsory Book 2, Chapters 5–6 (People's Education Press, 2019): motion and force, projectile motion, and circular motion."""
from phy_hs_lib import T, C, N

# ---------------- Compulsory Book 1, Chapter 4: Motion and Interactions
T("newton1", "Motion and interactions", "conceptual",
  "Understand Newton's first law: every object remains at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line unless an external force compels it to change that state. Understand the reasoning behind Galileo's ideal inclined-plane experiment, and recognize that force changes an object's state of motion rather than sustaining its motion.",
  ["State Newton's first law and explain the relationship between force and motion", "Describe the conclusion of Galileo's ideal inclined-plane experiment", "Recognize that Newton's first law follows from scientific reasoning and cannot be verified directly by experiment"],
  [("B1.2.3", "exact")],
  pre=[("J:newton1", "hard"), ("J:balance2", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "What state of motion will an object be in if no external force acts on it?", "It will remain at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line",
      ["It will always remain at rest", "Its speed will gradually decrease to zero", "It will move with constant acceleration in a straight line"],
      "Newton's first law: without an external force, an object remains at rest or continues in uniform motion in a straight line. An object already moving does not stop just because no force acts on it."),
    C("basic", "What does Galileo's ideal inclined-plane experiment and reasoning show?", "Force changes an object's state of motion; it is not needed to sustain the motion",
      ["Force sustains an object's motion, so it stops when the force is removed", "An object without a force must remain at rest and cannot move", "A moving object eventually stops because its inertia gradually disappears"],
      "As the incline becomes level and friction approaches zero, the ball continues moving indefinitely. This shows that force is not needed to sustain motion; an object moving on a horizontal surface eventually stops because friction acts on it."),
    C("standard", "Which statement about Newton's first law is correct?", "It follows from experimental evidence and scientific reasoning, and cannot be verified directly by experiment",
      ["It is merely a special case of Newton's second law when the net force is zero", "It can be verified directly because objects with no forces acting on them exist", "Only objects at rest obey Newton's first law"],
      "No object in reality is completely free of forces, so the first law cannot be verified directly by experiment. It reveals the property of inertia and identifies force as the cause of changes in motion; it is not merely a special case of the second law."),
    N("standard", "A ball moves to the right at 5 m/s across a smooth horizontal surface. At a certain instant, all forces on it suddenly disappear. What is the magnitude of its displacement during the next 2 s, in meters? (Enter the number only.)", "10",
      "Once all forces disappear, the ball continues at its original constant velocity. Its displacement is x = vt = 5 × 2 = 10 m. Assuming it gradually stops would give the wrong answer.", "5*2"),
  ])

T("inertia", "Motion and interactions", "conceptual",
  "Know that inertia is an object's tendency to remain at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line, and that all objects have inertia. Mass is the sole measure of inertia; inertia does not depend on speed, applied force, or location. Use inertia to explain everyday phenomena.",
  ["Know that inertia depends only on mass", "Use inertia to explain phenomena such as braking and starting", "Do not treat inertia as a force"],
  [("B1.2.3", "exact")],
  pre=[("newton1", "hard"), ("J:mass", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Which factor determines the amount of inertia an object has?", "The object's mass",
      ["The object's speed", "The net force on the object", "Whether the object is moving"],
      "Mass is the sole measure of inertia: the greater the mass, the greater the inertia and the harder it is to change the object's state of motion."),
    C("basic", "A car traveling on a straight road brakes suddenly, and the passenger's body leans forward. Why?", "The passenger's upper body tends to keep moving forward because of inertia",
      ["The passenger is acted on by a forward inertial force", "The car pushes the passenger forward as it brakes", "The passenger's inertia increases during braking"],
      "When the car brakes, the passenger's feet slow with it, but the upper body tends to keep moving at its original speed because of inertia, so the passenger leans forward. Inertia is not a force, and its magnitude does not change with speed."),
    C("standard", "Which statement about inertia is correct?", "An object in a space station still has inertia even when it is in free fall",
      ["An object with greater speed has greater inertia", "An object at rest has no inertia", "An object acted on by a greater force has greater inertia"],
      "Inertia depends only on mass, not on speed, whether a force acts, or whether the object is weightless. Every object has inertia in every state of motion."),
    C("standard", "A small ball rests on the smooth floor of a train car moving to the right at constant velocity. The train suddenly accelerates. How does an observer standing on the ground see the ball move?", "It continues moving to the right at its original constant velocity",
      ["It accelerates to the left", "It immediately stops in place", "It accelerates to the right with the train"],
      "The smooth floor exerts no horizontal force on the ball, so inertia keeps it moving at its original velocity. After the train accelerates, the ball moves backward relative to the train car, but continues to the right at constant velocity relative to the ground."),
  ])

T("exp_newton2", "Motion and interactions", "procedural",
  "Use a controlled-variable method with a cart, hanging masses, and a ticker timer to investigate how acceleration depends on net force and mass. Balance friction, keep the hanging mass much smaller than the cart's mass, use the ticker tape to find acceleration, and draw a-F and a-1/m graphs to reach a conclusion.",
  ["Know how to balance friction and why the hanging mass must be much smaller than the cart's mass", "Use the successive-difference method to find acceleration from ticker tape", "Analyze errors and conclusions from a-F and a-1/m graphs"],
  [("B1.X.4", "exact"), ("B1.2.3", "inferred")],
  pre=[("acceleration", "hard"), ("exp_vt", "hard"), ("inertia", "soft")],
  math=[("cnm_200", "a is directly proportional to F, so the a-F graph is a straight line through the origin (a direct-proportion function)."),
        ("cnm_238", "a is inversely proportional to m; plotting a against 1/m turns the curve into a straight line.")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "When investigating how acceleration depends on mass, which quantity should remain constant as the cart's mass changes?", "The tension in the string pulling the cart (the net force on the cart)",
      ["The cart's speed", "The distance traveled by the cart", "The cart's inertia"],
      "This is the controlled-variable method: keep net force F constant when studying how a depends on m, and keep mass m constant when studying how a depends on F."),
    C("basic", "Which is the correct way to balance friction?", "Remove the hanging mass, raise the end of the board farthest from the pulley, and gently push the cart so it moves at constant velocity while pulling the ticker tape",
      ["Attach the hanging mass and adjust the board until the cart moves down at constant velocity with the tape", "Keep the board horizontal and gently push the cart until it moves at constant velocity", "Remove the hanging mass and tape, then release the cart from rest at the top of the board"],
      "Do not attach the hanging mass when balancing friction, but keep the ticker tape attached so friction in the tape guide is also balanced. Adjust the board so the component of gravity along it equals friction. Once balanced, changing the cart's mass does not require balancing again."),
    C("standard", "A student plots a-F, taking F to be the weight mg of the hanging mass. The graph curves downward at its end as F increases. What is the main cause?", "The hanging mass is not much smaller than the cart's mass, so the string tension is less than the hanging mass's weight",
      ["Friction is overbalanced because the board is raised too high", "Friction has not been balanced", "The ticker timer's frequency is too low"],
      "The hanging mass and cart accelerate together, so the tension is T = Mmg/(M+m) < mg. When m is not much smaller than M, using mg on the horizontal axis makes the graph curve downward. Overbalancing friction gives a positive intercept on the a-axis; not balancing it gives a positive intercept on the F-axis. Neither makes the graph curve downward."),
    N("standard", "The time between adjacent marked points on ticker tape is T = 0.10 s. Four consecutive displacements are x₁ = 3.00 cm, x₂ = 3.38 cm, x₃ = 3.82 cm, and x₄ = 4.20 cm. Use the successive-difference method to find the magnitude of the acceleration in m/s². (Round to two decimal places; enter the number only.)", "0.41",
      "a = [(x₃+x₄) − (x₁+x₂)] / (4T²) = (8.02 − 6.38)×10⁻² / (4×0.10²) = 0.0164/0.04 = 0.41 m/s². Convert centimeters to meters.",
      "round(((3.82+4.20)-(3.00+3.38))*0.01/(4*0.1**2),2)"),
  ])

T("newton2", "Motion and interactions", "reasoning",
  "Understand Newton's second law: an object's acceleration is directly proportional to the net force on it and inversely proportional to its mass, and points in the direction of the net force: F_net = ma. Note the vector nature (a points in the direction of F_net), instantaneous nature (a and F_net arise, change, and disappear together), and same-object requirement (F, m, and a refer to the same object).",
  ["Explain the vector, instantaneous, and same-object requirements of F_net = ma", "Find the net force on an object and calculate its acceleration", "Analyze instantaneous acceleration when a spring or string force changes abruptly"],
  [("B1.2.3", "exact")],
  pre=[("acceleration", "hard"), ("inertia", "hard"), ("force_compose", "hard"), ("exp_newton2", "soft")],
  math=[("cnm_h079", "Acceleration and net force are both vectors. The direction of a matches F_net, so the vector equation F_net = ma relies on the concept of vectors.")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Which statement about Newton's second law is correct?", "An object's acceleration always points in the direction of the net force acting on it",
      ["An object's acceleration always points in the direction of its velocity", "A more massive object must have less acceleration than a less massive object", "A greater net force on an object always means a greater speed"],
      "a points in the direction of F_net, but it can point in the same or opposite direction as velocity, or at an angle to it. Acceleration also depends on F_net, not just mass; net force determines acceleration, not velocity."),
    N("basic", "A 4 kg block on a horizontal surface is pulled to the right by a 20 N force and experiences 8 N of sliding friction. What is the magnitude of its acceleration in m/s²? (Enter the number only.)", "3",
      "F_net = F − f = 20 − 8 = 12 N, and a = F_net/m = 12/4 = 3 m/s². In F = ma, F must be the net force; using 20/4 = 5 directly is incorrect.", "(20-8)/4"),
    C("standard", "Two balls A and B each have mass m. Ball A hangs from the ceiling by a light spring, and A and B are connected by a light string. The system is at rest. At the instant the string between A and B is cut, what are the accelerations aA and aB? (The gravitational acceleration is g.)",
      "aA = g upward; aB = g downward",
      ["aA = g downward; aB = g downward", "aA = 0; aB = g downward", "aA = 2g upward; aB = g downward"],
      "Before the cut, the spring force is 2mg. The spring's deformation cannot change instantly, so its force is unchanged: A has 2mg upward and mg downward, giving a net force mg upward and aA = g. The string tension on B disappears instantly, leaving only gravity, so aB = g downward."),
    N("standard", "A 5 kg object is at rest on a smooth horizontal surface and is acted on by two perpendicular horizontal forces: F₁ = 6 N east and F₂ = 8 N north. What is the magnitude of its acceleration in m/s²? (Enter the number only.)", "2",
      "The net force is a vector sum: F_net = √(6² + 8²) = 10 N, so a = 10/5 = 2 m/s², in the direction of the net force (north of east). Adding the force magnitudes to get 14 N would incorrectly give 2.8.",
      "sqrt(6**2+8**2)/5"),
  ])

T("units", "Motion and interactions", "conceptual",
  "Know the three base quantities in mechanics in the International System of Units (length, mass, and time) and their base units (meter, kilogram, and second). Know that derived units follow from physical equations; for example, 1 N = 1 kg·m/s². Use units to check equations and convert quantities.",
  ["Distinguish base units from derived units", "Convert units using 1 N = 1 kg·m/s²", "Use units to check whether an equation is reasonable"],
  [("B1.2.4", "exact"), ("B1.2.3", "inferred")],
  pre=[("newton2", "hard"), ("J:len_time", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Which of the following is a base unit for mechanics in the International System of Units?", "Kilogram",
      ["Newton", "Meter per second", "Gram"],
      "The base units for mechanics are the meter (m), kilogram (kg), and second (s). The newton and meter per second are derived units; the gram is not an SI base unit."),
    C("basic", "According to Newton's second law, F = ma. How is 1 N expressed using SI base units?", "1 kg·m/s²",
      ["1 kg·m/s", "1 kg·m²/s²", "1 kg/(m·s²)"],
      "A force that gives a 1 kg object an acceleration of 1 m/s² is 1 N, so 1 N = 1 kg·m/s². kg·m/s is the unit of momentum, and kg·m²/s² is the unit of energy (the joule)."),
    C("standard", "Let x denote displacement, v velocity, a acceleration, and t time. Which expression could be correct based on its units?", "x = v²/a",
      ["x = v t²", "x = a t", "x = v/a"],
      "The units of v²/a are (m/s)²/(m/s²) = m, matching displacement. The units of vt² are m·s, at are m/s, and v/a are s; none of these is a unit of length."),
    N("standard", "A 500 g object has an acceleration of 20 cm/s² due to the net horizontal force acting on it. What is the magnitude of the net force in N? (Enter the number only.)", "0.1",
      "Convert to SI units first: m = 0.5 kg and a = 0.2 m/s². Then F = ma = 0.5 × 0.2 = 0.1 N. Using 500 × 20 directly would incorrectly give 10000.", "0.5*0.2"),
  ])

T("dyn_apply", "Motion and interactions", "application",
  "Use Newton's second law and kinematics equations to solve two types of problems: find motion from known forces, or find forces from known motion. Analyze the forces, choose coordinates, find the net force, and use acceleration to connect force and motion. For incline and friction problems, resolve gravity into components.",
  ["Find acceleration from forces, then determine quantities of motion", "Find acceleration from motion, then determine a force", "Analyze forces on an incline and find the acceleration"],
  [("B1.2.3", "exact")],
  pre=[("newton2", "hard"), ("vx_formula", "hard"), ("kinetic_friction", "hard"), ("force_decompose", "hard")],
  qs=[
    N("basic", "A 1000 kg car travels at 20 m/s. After braking, it moves with constant deceleration and stops in 5 s. What is the magnitude of the resistive force on the car in N? (Enter the number only.)", "4000",
      "The magnitude of a = Δv/t = 20/5 = 4 m/s², so the resistive force is f = ma = 1000 × 4 = 4000 N.", "1000*20/5"),
    C("basic", "An object slides down a smooth incline at 30°. Take g = 10 m/s². What is the magnitude of its acceleration? (sin30° = 0.5, cos30° ≈ 0.87)", "5 m/s²",
      ["10 m/s²", "8.7 m/s²", "5.8 m/s²"],
      "Along the incline, mg sin30° = ma, so a = g sin30° = 5 m/s². 10 assumes a = g; 8.7 uses cos instead of sin; 5.8 incorrectly uses g tan30°.", verify="10*0.5"),
    N("standard", "A block slides down a 37° incline. The kinetic-friction coefficient between the block and the incline is 0.5. Take g = 10 m/s², sin37° = 0.6, and cos37° = 0.8. What is the magnitude of the block's acceleration in m/s²? (Enter the number only.)", "2",
      "Along the incline, mg sin37° − μmg cos37° = ma, so a = g(sin37° − μ cos37°) = 10 × (0.6 − 0.5×0.8) = 2 m/s².", "10*(0.6-0.5*0.8)"),
    N("advanced", "A block is launched up a 37° incline at 10 m/s. The kinetic-friction coefficient between the block and the incline is 0.5. Take g = 10 m/s², sin37° = 0.6, and cos37° = 0.8. What is the maximum distance the block travels up the incline, in meters? (Enter the number only.)", "5",
      "As the block moves up, friction acts down the incline: a = g(sin37° + μ cos37°) = 10 × (0.6 + 0.4) = 10 m/s², so the motion is uniformly decelerated. From v² = 2ax, x = 10²/(2×10) = 5 m. Treating friction as acting upward would give a = 2 and the wrong result.",
      "10**2/(2*10*(0.6+0.5*0.8))"),
  ])

T("connected", "Motion and interactions", "modeling",
  "Analyze systems of connected objects: when they share an acceleration, use the system approach to find it, then isolate individual objects to find interaction forces. Determine string tension and friction between stacked objects, including the threshold condition for no relative slipping.",
  ["Use the system approach to find a system's acceleration", "Isolate objects to find internal forces such as string tension and contact forces", "Use maximum static friction to find the threshold for stacked objects to move without slipping relative to each other"],
  [("B1.2.3", "exact")],
  pre=[("dyn_apply", "hard"), ("newton3", "hard"), ("static_friction", "soft")],
  qs=[
    N("basic", "Two objects with masses m₁ = 3 kg and m₂ = 2 kg are connected by a light string on a smooth horizontal surface. A horizontal force F = 20 N pulls m₂, causing both objects to move together. What is their acceleration in m/s²? (Enter the number only.)", "4",
      "Both objects have the same acceleration, so treat them as one system: F = (m₁ + m₂)a, and a = 20/(3+2) = 4 m/s².", "20/(3+2)"),
    C("basic", "Two objects with masses m₁ = 3 kg and m₂ = 2 kg are connected by a light string on a smooth horizontal surface. A horizontal force F = 20 N pulls m₂, causing both objects to move together. What is the tension in the string connecting them?", "12 N",
      ["20 N", "8 N", "10 N"],
      "Isolate m₁: T = m₁a = 3 × 4 = 12 N. 20 N treats the string tension as the external force F; 8 N uses m₂a; 10 N incorrectly splits F equally.", verify="3*20/(3+2)"),
    C("standard", "A 4 kg block rests on a smooth horizontal table and is connected by a light string over a smooth fixed pulley to a hanging 1 kg block. Take g = 10 m/s². What is the string tension after release?", "8 N",
      ["10 N", "2 N", "40 N"],
      "For the system, m₂g = (m₁ + m₂)a, so a = 2 m/s². For m₁, T = m₁a = 8 N (or use m₂g − T = m₂a to get T = 8 N). 10 N incorrectly assumes the tension equals m₂g; m₂ accelerates downward, so T < m₂g.",
      verify="4*1*10/(4+1)"),
    N("advanced", "A 1 kg block A rests on a 2 kg board B, which is on a smooth horizontal surface. The kinetic-friction coefficient between A and B is 0.2, and the maximum static friction equals the kinetic friction. Take g = 10 m/s². What is the largest horizontal force F that can be applied to B so A and B remain at rest relative to each other, in N? (Enter the number only.)", "6",
      "The only force accelerating A is static friction from B, so the maximum acceleration is a = μg = 2 m/s². For the system, F = (m_A + m_B)a = 3 × 2 = 6 N. Using F = μm_Ag = 2 N would count only the maximum static friction on A and forget that F must also accelerate B.",
      "(1+2)*0.2*10"),
  ])

T("weightless", "Motion and interactions", "application",
  "Understand apparent weight and weightlessness: an object's force on a support (or tension in a suspension) greater than its weight indicates an overweight condition; a smaller force indicates weightlessness; and zero force indicates complete weightlessness. The direction of acceleration determines the condition: upward acceleration causes apparent overweight, downward acceleration causes weightlessness, and downward acceleration a = g causes complete weightlessness. Gravity itself does not change.",
  ["Determine apparent overweight or weightlessness from the acceleration direction", "Calculate apparent weight during apparent overweight or weightlessness", "Know that gravity still acts during complete weightlessness"],
  [("B1.2.3", "exact")],
  pre=[("newton2", "hard"), ("dyn_apply", "soft"), ("free_fall", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Which statement is correct when an object is in an overweight condition?", "The object's force on its support is greater than its weight",
      ["The object's weight has increased", "The object's mass has increased", "The object must be moving upward"],
      "An overweight condition means apparent weight (the support force or tension) exceeds true weight; neither gravity nor mass changes. It depends on upward acceleration, not upward velocity, so an object slowing while descending is also overweight."),
    N("basic", "A 60 kg person stands on a scale in an elevator accelerating upward at 2 m/s². Take g = 10 m/s². What is the scale reading (the force the person exerts on the scale), in N? (Enter the number only.)", "720",
      "Take upward as positive: N − mg = ma, so N = m(g + a) = 60 × 12 = 720 N. This is greater than the person's 600 N weight, so the person is overweight.", "60*(10+2)"),
    C("standard", "Neglecting air resistance, which object is in a state of complete weightlessness?", "A stone moving as a projectile through the air",
      ["An object in an elevator moving upward at constant velocity", "A child on a swing passing its lowest point", "A person in a parachute descending at constant velocity"],
      "The stone is acted on only by gravity, so its acceleration is g downward and it is completely weightless. An object moving at constant velocity has zero acceleration, so it is neither overweight nor weightless; at the lowest point of a swing, centripetal acceleration is upward, causing an overweight condition."),
    C("advanced", "A 2 kg object hangs from a spring scale in an elevator. The scale reads 16 N. Take g = 10 m/s². What is the elevator's acceleration?", "Magnitude 2 m/s², vertically downward",
      ["Magnitude 2 m/s², vertically upward", "Magnitude 8 m/s², vertically downward", "Magnitude 8 m/s², vertically upward"],
      "The 16 N reading is less than the 20 N weight, indicating weightlessness with downward acceleration. mg − F = ma, so a = (20 − 16)/2 = 2 m/s². The elevator could be accelerating downward or slowing while moving upward."),
  ])

# ---------------- Compulsory Book 2, Chapter 5: Projectile Motion
T("curve_motion", "Motion and interactions", "conceptual",
  "Know that a particle's velocity at a point on a curved path is tangent to the path and that curved motion is always changing motion. Understand that an object follows a curved path when the net force is not along the velocity; the net force points toward the concave side of the trajectory.",
  ["Know that velocity in curved motion is tangent to the path", "Identify the condition for an object to follow a curved path", "Determine the net-force direction from the direction of the curve"],
  [("B2.2.1", "exact")],
  pre=[("velocity", "hard"), ("newton2", "hard")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "At a point on a particle's curved path, what is the direction of its velocity?", "Tangent to the path at that point",
      ["Normal to the path at that point (perpendicular to it)", "Toward the inside of the curve", "In the same direction as the acceleration at that point"],
      "A particle's instantaneous velocity at a point is tangent to its path there; the velocity and acceleration directions are generally different."),
    C("basic", "What condition causes an object to follow a curved path?", "The net force is not along the velocity",
      ["The net force is changing", "The net force is nonzero", "The speed is constantly changing"],
      "When the net force is along the velocity, motion is in a straight line (speeding up or slowing down); when it is not, motion is curved. A constant force can also produce curved motion, as in projectile motion, so the force need not change. A nonzero net force is only a necessary condition: if it is along the velocity, the path is still straight."),
    C("standard", "Which statement about a particle moving along a curved path is correct?", "Its velocity direction must change, so it is in changing motion",
      ["Its acceleration must change", "Its net force must change", "Its speed must change"],
      "The velocity direction changes continuously during curved motion, so the motion is changing. Projectile motion has constant acceleration, and uniform circular motion has constant speed, so the other statements are not necessarily true."),
    C("standard", "In a horizontal plane, a particle moves along a curve from M through P to N. At P its velocity points east, the path curves north, and its speed is increasing. Which direction could the net force at P point?", "Northeast (both eastward and northward)",
      ["Southeast", "Northwest", "Due east"],
      "The net force points toward the concave side of the path, so it has a northward component. Since the speed is increasing, the force also has an eastward component along the velocity, making it point northeast. Northwest would reduce the speed, southeast would curve the path south, and due east would not curve the path."),
  ])

T("motion_compose", "Motion and interactions", "modeling",
  "Understand combined motion and component motions: component motions are independent and occur over the same time interval, while displacement, velocity, and acceleration combine by the parallelogram rule. Use vector addition and resolution to analyze problems such as a boat crossing a river, and determine whether the combined path is straight or curved.",
  ["Know that combined motion and component motions are simultaneous and independent", "Use the parallelogram rule to find resultant velocity and displacement", "Analyze the shortest time and shortest distance for a boat crossing a river"],
  [("B2.2.2", "exact"), ("B2.2.1", "inferred")],
  pre=[("curve_motion", "hard"), ("displacement", "hard"), ("force_compose", "soft")],
  math=[("cnm_174", "Use the Pythagorean theorem to find the resultant velocity from two perpendicular component velocities.")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Which statement about combined motion and component motions is correct?", "Combined motion and each component motion last for the same amount of time",
      ["The speed of the combined motion must be greater than each component speed", "The component motions affect each other", "The magnitude of the combined displacement must equal the sum of the component displacement magnitudes"],
      "Component motions and combined motion begin and end at the same time, and the component motions do not affect one another. Velocity and displacement combine by the parallelogram rule; the resultant magnitude can be smaller than a component, so magnitudes cannot simply be added."),
    N("basic", "A boat moves at 4 m/s in still water, and the river current is 3 m/s. The boat points perpendicular to the riverbank. What is its speed relative to the bank, in m/s? (Enter the number only.)", "5",
      "The boat's velocity and the current are perpendicular, so the resultant velocity is v = √(4² + 3²) = 5 m/s. Do not add them directly to get 7.", "sqrt(4**2+3**2)"),
    C("standard", "An object simultaneously undergoes uniform horizontal motion and vertically accelerated motion from rest. What is its combined motion?", "Uniformly accelerated curved motion",
      ["Uniform straight-line motion", "Uniformly accelerated straight-line motion", "Nonuniformly accelerated curved motion"],
      "The initial resultant velocity is horizontal, while the constant resultant acceleration is vertical. Since they are not along the same line, the path is curved; constant acceleration makes it uniformly accelerated curved motion, as in projectile motion."),
    N("advanced", "A river is 200 m wide. A boat moves at 5 m/s in still water, and the current is 3 m/s. To arrive directly opposite, the boat must point upstream. How long does the crossing take, in seconds? (Enter the number only.)", "50",
      "Pointing upstream makes the boat's along-river velocity component cancel the current. Its cross-river component is v = √(5² − 3²) = 4 m/s, so t = d/v = 200/4 = 50 s. Using 200/5 = 40 s directly is incorrect.",
      "200/sqrt(5**2-3**2)"),
  ])

T("exp_projectile", "Motion and interactions", "procedural",
  "Investigate projectile motion experimentally: strike a metal plate with a small hammer so two balls begin falling at the same time, demonstrating that the vertical component is free fall. Use a ramp and coordinate paper to plot the trajectory, and find the initial velocity from the trajectory or a stroboscopic photograph. Keep the end of the ramp horizontal and release the ball from the same point each time.",
  ["Know how to adjust the projectile-motion apparatus", "Explain what is shown when the two balls land at the same time", "Find the initial velocity of a projectile from points on its trajectory"],
  [("B2.X.2", "exact"), ("B2.2.2", "exact")],
  pre=[("curve_motion", "hard"), ("free_fall", "hard"), ("motion_compose", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "In an experiment investigating projectile motion, why should the tangent to the ramp be horizontal at its end?", "To ensure that the ball leaves the ramp with a horizontal velocity",
      ["To reduce friction between the ball and the ramp", "To increase the ball's speed as it leaves the ramp", "To make the ball land farther away"],
      "A horizontal ramp end gives the ball a horizontal initial velocity so it follows projectile motion. The ramp does not need to be frictionless as long as the release conditions are the same each time."),
    C("basic", "Why must the ball be released from rest at the same point on the ramp every time?", "To give the ball the same initial velocity on every trial",
      ["To make the ball's time in the air the same every time", "To reduce the effect of air resistance", "To make the ball's mass the same"],
      "When released from rest at the same point, the ball reaches the end at the same speed each time. The trajectories then overlap, so the plotted points lie on the same path."),
    N("standard", "A stroboscopic photograph records a projectile at points A, B, and C, with equal time intervals between adjacent points. The horizontal spacing between adjacent points is 0.30 m; the vertical spacings AB and BC are 0.15 m and 0.25 m. Take g = 10 m/s². What is the ball's initial horizontal velocity in m/s? (Enter the number only.)", "3",
      "Vertically, Δy = gT²: 0.25 − 0.15 = 10T², so T = 0.1 s. Horizontally, the velocity is constant: v₀ = x/T = 0.30/0.1 = 3 m/s.",
      "round(0.30/sqrt((0.25-0.15)/10),2)"),
    C("standard", "A small hammer strikes an elastic metal plate as ball A is launched horizontally and ball B is dropped in free fall. The balls always land at the same time. What does this show?", "The vertical component of projectile motion is free fall",
      ["The vertical component of projectile motion is uniform straight-line motion", "The horizontal component of projectile motion is free fall", "The time a projectile spends in the air depends on its initial speed"],
      "Since A and B land together, the vertical component of A's motion is the same as B's free fall. This experiment does not show the nature of the horizontal component; its uniform motion must be verified separately using the trajectory."),
  ])

T("projectile", "Motion and interactions", "modeling",
  "Understand projectile motion: horizontally, the object moves at constant velocity, x = v₀t; vertically, it is in free fall, y = gt²/2. The time to land depends only on the drop height. Find velocity and displacement by resolving and combining components; for a projectile landing on an incline, use the angle between its displacement and the horizontal.",
  ["Find a projectile's flight time, range, and landing velocity", "Use vy/vx and y/x to express the directions of velocity and displacement", "Solve projectile-motion problems involving an inclined landing surface"],
  [("B2.2.2", "exact")],
  pre=[("motion_compose", "hard"), ("free_fall", "hard"), ("exp_projectile", "soft")],
  math=[("cnm_276", "Use tangent for the angle between velocity or displacement and the horizontal: tanθ = vertical component / horizontal component.")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Neglecting air resistance, what determines the time a projectile remains in the air?", "The height from which it falls",
      ["Its initial speed", "Its mass", "Both its initial speed and the drop height"],
      "Vertically, h = gt²/2, so t = √(2h/g) and depends only on the drop height. The initial speed affects only the horizontal range."),
    N("basic", "A ball is launched horizontally at 10 m/s from a height of 45 m. Take g = 10 m/s² and neglect air resistance. How far horizontally from the launch point does it land, in meters? (Enter the number only.)", "30",
      "t = √(2h/g) = √(2×45/10) = 3 s, so x = v₀t = 10 × 3 = 30 m.", "10*sqrt(2*45/10)"),
    N("standard", "An object is launched horizontally at 40 m/s from a height of 45 m. Take g = 10 m/s² and neglect air resistance. What is its speed when it lands, in m/s? (Enter the number only.)", "50",
      "The landing time is t = 3 s, the vertical velocity is vy = gt = 30 m/s, and the horizontal velocity is 40 m/s. The resultant speed is v = √(40² + 30²) = 50 m/s. Adding vx and vy directly would give the incorrect result 70.",
      "sqrt(40**2+(10*3)**2)"),
    N("advanced", "A ball is launched horizontally at 8 m/s from the top of a 37° incline and lands on the incline. Take g = 10 m/s² and tan37° = 0.75. How long is the ball in the air, in seconds? (Enter the number only.)", "1.2",
      "When the ball lands on the incline, the angle of its displacement from the horizontal equals the incline angle: tan37° = (gt²/2)/(v₀t) = gt/(2v₀), so t = 2v₀tan37°/g = 2 × 8 × 0.75/10 = 1.2 s.",
      "2*8*0.75/10"),
  ])

T("oblique", "Motion and interactions", "modeling",
  "Resolve oblique projectile motion into uniform horizontal motion (vx = v₀cosθ) and uniformly accelerated vertical motion (vy = v₀sinθ − gt). At the highest point, the velocity is horizontal but nonzero. Find the maximum height, range, and flight time, and know that complementary launch angles produce the same range.",
  ["Resolve the initial velocity into horizontal and vertical components", "Find the ascent time, maximum height, and range of oblique projectile motion", "Know that the velocity at the highest point is nonzero while acceleration remains g"],
  [("B2.2.2", "inferred")],
  pre=[("projectile", "hard"), ("motion_compose", "soft"), ("vt_formula", "soft")],
  math=[("cnm_277", "Resolve the initial velocity using sine and cosine values for special angles.")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Neglecting air resistance, an object is launched at initial speed v₀ at an angle θ above the horizontal. Which statement is correct when it reaches its highest point?", "Its velocity is horizontal with magnitude v₀cosθ, and its acceleration is still g",
      ["Its velocity is zero and its acceleration is g", "Its velocity is horizontal with magnitude v₀cosθ, and its acceleration is zero", "Its speed is still v₀ and its acceleration is g"],
      "Horizontal velocity is constant, so the horizontal velocity at the highest point is v₀cosθ ≠ 0. The vertical velocity becomes zero, but acceleration remains g downward."),
    N("basic", "A shell is launched upward at 50 m/s at 37° above the horizontal. Take g = 10 m/s², neglect air resistance, and use sin37° = 0.6 and cos37° = 0.8. How long does it take to reach its highest point, in seconds? (Enter the number only.)", "3",
      "The initial vertical velocity is vy = v₀sin37° = 30 m/s, so t = vy/g = 30/10 = 3 s.", "50*0.6/10"),
    N("standard", "A shell is launched upward at 50 m/s at 37° above the horizontal and lands at the same height. Take g = 10 m/s², neglect air resistance, and use sin37° = 0.6 and cos37° = 0.8. What is its horizontal range in meters? (Enter the number only.)", "240",
      "The ascent and descent times are equal, so the total time is T = 2 × 3 = 6 s. The horizontal velocity is vx = 50 × 0.8 = 40 m/s, giving a range x = vxT = 40 × 6 = 240 m.", "2*50*0.8*(50*0.6/10)"),
    C("advanced", "Two objects are launched upward with the same initial speed at angles of 30° and 60° above the horizontal. They land at the same height and air resistance is neglected. Which statement is correct?", "They have the same range, and the 60° launch reaches a greater height",
      ["They have the same range, and the 30° launch reaches a greater height", "The 60° launch has a greater range", "The 30° launch has a greater range"],
      "The range is R = v₀²sin2θ/g; sin60° = sin120°, so the ranges are equal. The maximum height is H = v₀²sin²θ/(2g), which increases with θ, so the 60° launch reaches a greater height."),
  ])

# ---------------- Compulsory Book 2, Chapter 6: Circular Motion
T("circular", "Motion and interactions", "procedural",
  "Describe uniform circular motion using linear velocity, angular velocity, and period: v = Δs/Δt, ω = Δθ/Δt (θ in radians, in rad/s), T = 2π/ω, v = ωr, and ω = 2πn. Know that the direction of linear velocity changes continuously, so uniform circular motion is changing motion. Analyze belt drives and objects rotating about the same axis.",
  ["Calculate using the relationships among v, ω, T, and n, converting rotational-speed units as needed", "Know that speed stays constant while velocity direction changes in uniform circular motion", "Compare v and ω in belt-drive and same-axis rotation"],
  [("B2.2.3", "exact")],
  pre=[("velocity", "hard"), ("curve_motion", "hard"), ("J:speed", "soft")],
  math=[("cnm_h057", "Angular velocity is expressed in radians; one revolution is 2π rad."),
        ("cnm_h059", "The arc-length formula s = rθ is the mathematical basis for v = ωr.")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Which statement about uniform circular motion is correct?", "The linear speed is constant, but its direction changes continuously, so the motion is changing",
      ["The linear velocity is constant, so the motion is uniform", "The angular velocity changes continuously", "The period changes continuously"],
      "“Uniform” means constant speed. Linear velocity is a vector whose tangential direction changes continuously, so uniform circular motion is changing motion; angular velocity and period remain constant."),
    N("basic", "A particle moves in a circle of radius 0.5 m with angular velocity 4 rad/s. What is its linear speed in m/s? (Enter the number only.)", "2",
      "v = ωr = 4 × 0.5 = 2 m/s.", "4*0.5"),
    N("standard", "A flywheel rotates at 180 r/min. What is its angular velocity in rad/s? (Use π = 3.14, round to two decimal places, and enter the number only.)", "18.84",
      "First convert the rotational speed: n = 180/60 = 3 r/s. Then ω = 2πn = 2 × 3.14 × 3 = 18.84 rad/s. Substituting 180 directly as r/s would give the incorrect result 1130.4.",
      "round(2*3.14*180/60,2)"),
    C("standard", "In a belt drive with no slipping, the driver has twice the radius of the follower. What is the ratio of their angular velocities at the rims, ω_driver:ω_follower?", "1:2",
      ["2∶1", "1∶1", "1∶4"],
      "With no slipping, the linear speeds at the rims are equal. From v = ωr, angular velocity is inversely proportional to radius, so ω_driver:ω_follower = r_follower:r_driver = 1:2. Objects rotating about the same axis have equal angular velocities."),
  ])

T("centripetal_force", "Motion and interactions", "reasoning",
  "Know that uniform circular motion requires a net force directed toward the center, providing the centripetal force, with magnitude F = mv²/r = mω²r. “Centripetal force” names a role played by one force or the resultant of several forces; it is not an additional type of force and should not be added separately to a force diagram.",
  ["Know that centripetal force describes an effect rather than a new type of force", "Calculate using F = mv²/r and F = mω²r", "Identify the source of centripetal force in a given situation"],
  [("B2.2.3", "exact")],
  pre=[("circular", "hard"), ("newton2", "hard")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Which statement about centripetal force is correct?", "Centripetal force describes a role played by one force or by the resultant of several forces acting on an object",
      ["Centripetal force is an additional type of force acting on an object in circular motion", "Centripetal force can only be provided by elastic force", "Centripetal force is always equal to the object's weight"],
      "Centripetal force is not a new type of force. It may be provided by gravity, elastic force, friction, or their resultant. Draw only the actual forces in a force diagram; do not add a separate centripetal force."),
    N("basic", "A 0.5 kg ball moves in a horizontal circle at 4 m/s with a radius of 0.8 m. What is the required centripetal force in N? (Enter the number only.)", "10",
      "F = mv²/r = 0.5 × 4²/0.8 = 10 N. Forgetting to square the speed would give 2.5.", "0.5*4**2/0.8"),
    C("standard", "An object rests on a horizontal turntable and moves with it in a circle without slipping. What provides the centripetal force on the object?", "Static friction from the turntable",
      ["Kinetic friction from the turntable", "The resultant of gravity and the support force", "A centripetal force acting as a fourth force"],
      "Gravity and the support force balance, giving zero net vertical force. Since the object does not slip relative to the turntable, static friction directed toward the center provides the centripetal force."),
    C("standard", "An object of constant mass moves in a circle at constant speed. If its speed stays the same while its radius is halved, how does the required centripetal force change?", "It doubles",
      ["It quadruples", "It is halved", "It becomes one-quarter as large"],
      "From F = mv²/r, with v constant, F is inversely proportional to r. Halving r doubles F. If angular velocity were constant instead, F would be proportional to r, giving the opposite result.", verify="2"),
  ])

T("exp_centripetal", "Motion and interactions", "procedural",
  "Use a centripetal-force apparatus (stepped pulleys, a ruler, and a spring-force sleeve) to investigate how centripetal force depends on mass, angular velocity, and radius. Use a controlled-variable method; infer the force ratio from the exposed ruler marks and the angular-velocity ratio from the pulley-radius ratio.",
  ["Explain the controlled-variable method", "Find angular-velocity and centripetal-force ratios from the pulley-radius ratio", "Analyze experimental data using F = mω²r"],
  [("B2.X.3", "exact"), ("B2.2.3", "inferred")],
  pre=[("centripetal_force", "hard"), ("circular", "soft"), ("exp_newton2", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "When investigating how centripetal force depends on angular velocity, which quantities should be held constant?", "The ball's mass and radius of rotation",
      ["The ball's mass and linear speed", "The radius of rotation and linear speed", "The ball's mass and centripetal force"],
      "Use the controlled-variable method: to study how F depends on ω, keep m and r constant and change ω by changing the pulley-radius ratio."),
    C("basic", "In a centripetal-force apparatus, how is the ratio of the centripetal forces on two balls determined?", "From the ratio of the exposed ruler divisions on the left and right",
      ["From the ratio of the squares of the two spring extensions", "From the ratio of the number of turns of the handle", "From the ratio of the radii of the left and right stepped pulleys"],
      "The centripetal force on each ball is provided by compression of a spring in the force sleeve. The compression is shown by the exposed ruler marks, so the ratio of the marks equals the ratio of the forces."),
    C("standard", "In a centripetal-force apparatus, the radii of the belt-connected left and right stepped pulleys are in a 3:1 ratio. The balls have equal masses and equal rotation radii. What is the ratio of the exposed ruler divisions on the left and right?", "1:9",
      ["1∶3", "3∶1", "9∶1"],
      "The belt gives both pulleys the same rim speed, so ω is inversely proportional to radius and ω_left:ω_right = 1:3. With equal m and r, F is proportional to ω², so F_left:F_right = 1:9."),
    N("standard", "In an experiment, a ball has mass 0.1 kg and rotation radius 0.05 m, and the ruler indicates a centripetal force of 2.0 N. What is the ball's angular velocity in rad/s? (Enter the number only.)", "20",
      "From F = mω²r, ω = √(F/(mr)) = √(2.0/(0.1 × 0.05)) = √400 = 20 rad/s.", "round(sqrt(2.0/(0.1*0.05)),2)"),
  ])

T("centripetal_accel", "Motion and interactions", "reasoning",
  "Know that the acceleration in uniform circular motion always points toward the center; this is centripetal acceleration, with magnitude aₙ = v²/r = ω²r = 4π²r/T². Its direction changes continuously, so the motion is nonuniformly accelerated. Relate centripetal force and centripetal acceleration using Newton's second law.",
  ["Know the direction and meaning of centripetal acceleration", "Calculate using aₙ = v²/r = ω²r", "Compare centripetal acceleration at different points in a rotating system"],
  [("B2.2.3", "exact")],
  pre=[("circular", "hard"), ("centripetal_force", "hard"), ("acceleration", "soft")],
  math=[("cnm_h082", "Use vector subtraction to find the change in velocity Δv when deriving the direction of centripetal acceleration.")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "What is the direction of centripetal acceleration for an object in uniform circular motion?", "It always points toward the center and changes direction continuously",
      ["It points in the same direction as the linear velocity", "It is tangent to the path", "It points toward the center and has a constant direction"],
      "Centripetal acceleration changes only the direction of velocity. It always points toward the center, perpendicular to the linear velocity, and its direction changes as the object moves, so the motion is nonuniformly accelerated."),
    N("basic", "A particle moves in a circle at 6 m/s with a radius of 3 m. What is the magnitude of its centripetal acceleration in m/s²? (Enter the number only.)", "12",
      "aₙ = v²/r = 36/3 = 12 m/s².", "6**2/3"),
    C("standard", "In a belt drive with no slipping, wheel A has three times the radius of wheel B. What is the ratio of their centripetal accelerations at the rims, aA:aB?", "1:3",
      ["3∶1", "1∶9", "1∶1"],
      "The rim speeds are equal, and aₙ = v²/r is inversely proportional to r, so aA:aB = rB:rA = 1:3. The 3:1 choice assumes equal angular velocities (aₙ = ω²r); 1:9 compares only ω² and ignores the radius, or assumes aₙ is inversely proportional to r²."),
    N("standard", "A particle moves in a circle at 120 r/min with a radius of 0.25 m. What is its centripetal acceleration in m/s²? (Use π = 3.14, round to two decimal places, and enter the number only.)", "39.44",
      "n = 120/60 = 2 r/s, ω = 2πn = 12.56 rad/s, and aₙ = ω²r = 12.56² × 0.25 = 39.44 m/s². Failing to convert the rotational speed to r/s gives a much larger incorrect result.",
      "round((2*3.14*120/60)**2*0.25,2)"),
  ])

T("circular_apply", "Motion and interactions", "application",
  "Use Newton's second law to analyze everyday circular motion, such as cars crossing convex and concave bridges, vehicles turning on level roads or trains turning, and a ball on a string moving in a vertical circle. Identify the center of the circular path and write the equation along the radius: F_net = mv²/r.",
  ["Analyze forces on a car crossing a convex or concave bridge and compare the support force with weight", "Find the maximum speed for a turn on a level road", "Analyze the forces on a ball at the top of a vertical circle"],
  [("B2.2.3", "exact")],
  pre=[("centripetal_accel", "hard"), ("centripetal_force", "hard"), ("static_friction", "soft"), ("weightless", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "A car passes over the top of a convex bridge of radius r at speed v, where v < √(gr). What is the car's force on the bridge?", "Less than the car's weight; the car is weightless",
      ["Greater than the car's weight; the car is overweight", "Equal to the car's weight, regardless of speed", "Zero; the car is completely weightless"],
      "At the top, the center of the circle is below the car: mg − N = mv²/r, so N = mg − mv²/r < mg, indicating weightlessness. N is zero only when v = √(gr); since v < √(gr), the force is not zero."),
    N("basic", "A 1000 kg car passes the lowest point of a concave bridge of radius 50 m at 10 m/s. Take g = 10 m/s². What is the car's force on the bridge in N? (Enter the number only.)", "12000",
      "At the lowest point, the center of the circle is above the car: N − mg = mv²/r, so N = mg + mv²/r = 10000 + 1000×100/50 = 12000 N, indicating an overweight condition. Using mg − mv²/r would incorrectly give 8000.",
      "1000*10+1000*10**2/50"),
    N("standard", "A car turns on a level road with a radius of 20 m. The kinetic-friction coefficient between the tires and road is 0.5, and the maximum static friction equals the kinetic friction. Take g = 10 m/s². What is the maximum speed, in m/s, at which the car can turn without skidding? (Enter the number only.)", "10",
      "Static friction provides the centripetal force. At the limit, μmg = mv²/r, so v = √(μgr) = √(0.5 × 10 × 20) = 10 m/s, independent of the car's mass.", "sqrt(0.5*10*20)"),
    N("advanced", "A 0.5 kg ball attached to a 0.9 m light string moves in a vertical circle. At the highest point, its speed is 6 m/s. Take g = 10 m/s². What is the string tension at that instant, in N? (Enter the number only.)", "15",
      "At the highest point, both tension and gravity point downward toward the center: T + mg = mv²/r. Thus T = 0.5 × 36/0.9 − 0.5 × 10 = 20 − 5 = 15 N.", "0.5*6**2/0.9-0.5*10"),
  ])

T("centrifugal", "Motion and interactions", "conceptual",
  "Know that centrifugal motion is motion away from the center when the net force is insufficient to provide the required centripetal force, or when the net force disappears. There is no “centrifugal force.” Analyze examples such as spin-drying and skidding while turning, and distinguish uses of centrifugal effects from ways to prevent them.",
  ["Explain the conditions for centrifugal motion and know that there is no centrifugal force", "Predict an object's motion after a string breaks or it slips on a turntable", "Distinguish applications of centrifugal effects from ways to prevent them"],
  [("B2.2.3", "exact")],
  pre=[("centripetal_force", "hard"), ("circular_apply", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Why does an object move centrifugally?", "The net force is insufficient to provide the required centripetal force",
      ["The object experiences a centrifugal force directed radially outward", "The centripetal force on the object suddenly becomes too large", "The object's mass decreases"],
      "Centrifugal motion occurs when the net force is less than the required centripetal force, or zero. There is no agent exerting a “centrifugal force,” and the cause is not an excessive centripetal force."),
    C("basic", "A ball moves in a circle on a smooth horizontal table, with string tension providing the centripetal force. The string suddenly breaks. What does the ball do next?", "Move in a straight line at constant speed tangent to the circle",
      ["Fly outward along the radius", "Continue moving in a circle", "Move inward along the radius"],
      "After the string breaks, no horizontal force acts on the ball. Inertia preserves its instantaneous velocity, so it moves at constant speed along the tangent, not along the radius."),
    C("standard", "Which of the following prevents the harmful effects of centrifugal motion?", "Limiting vehicle speed on highway curves",
      ["Using a washing machine's spin cycle to dry clothes", "Separating blood cells with a centrifuge", "Making cotton candy with a cotton-candy machine"],
      "If a vehicle takes a turn too fast, friction may not provide enough centripetal force and the vehicle can skid, so speed limits are needed. The other examples make use of centrifugal effects."),
    N("standard", "An object sits on a horizontal turntable 0.5 m from the axis. The kinetic-friction coefficient between the object and turntable is 0.2, and the maximum static friction equals the kinetic friction. Take g = 10 m/s². Above what angular velocity, in rad/s, does the object begin to slip? (Enter the number only.)", "2",
      "At the threshold, maximum static friction provides the centripetal force: μmg = mω²r, so ω = √(μg/r) = √(0.2 × 10/0.5) = 2 rad/s. This is independent of mass, so objects at the same position begin to slip at the same angular velocity.",
      "sqrt(0.2*10/0.5)"),
  ])
